1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:05,984
This is a real camera on a NASA spacecraft,
closing in on an asteroid called Bennu.

2
00:00:06,218 --> 00:00:08,585
These are still photos, sped up.

3
00:00:08,760 --> 00:00:10,953
That round thing is the sampler head.

4
00:00:11,129 --> 00:00:16,554
The plan was simple: touch the surface,
blow in some gas, catch the loose material,

5
00:00:16,574 --> 00:00:20,137
and back away. But the
surface barely pushed back.

6
00:00:20,370 --> 00:00:24,971
The sampler's arm kept sinking into the
asteroid, and it was still going down when

7
00:00:24,991 --> 00:00:28,008
the thrusters fired to
pull the spacecraft away.

8
00:00:28,555 --> 00:00:34,456
So why did a world that looks like
solid rock behave almost like a

9
00:00:34,476 --> 00:00:40,303
pit of loose gravel? The
spacecraft was OSIRIS-REx,

10
00:00:40,432 --> 00:00:45,695
and its job was to bring a piece of an
asteroid back to Earth, where labs can study

11
00:00:45,715 --> 00:00:51,159
it in detail. Up close, Bennu turned
out to be crowded with boulders.

12
00:00:51,392 --> 00:00:56,767
So the team picked a small target between
them, a site they named Nightingale.

13
00:00:57,220 --> 00:01:02,390
The tool for the job is called TAGSAM,
short for Touch-And-Go Sample Acquisition

14
00:01:02,410 --> 00:01:06,971
Mechanism. It's not a drill, and
the spacecraft does not land.

15
00:01:07,146 --> 00:01:10,036
It's a round head on
the end of a long arm.

16
00:01:10,130 --> 00:01:14,615
The head touches the surface, and a burst
of gas pushes loose material up into a

17
00:01:14,635 --> 00:01:19,510
collector. On October
20, 2020, it was time.

18
00:01:19,801 --> 00:01:21,960
But nobody on Earth was steering.

19
00:01:22,193 --> 00:01:26,097
Before the event, NASA said a signal
would need about 18 and a half minutes to

20
00:01:26,117 --> 00:01:28,995
travel one way between
Earth and the spacecraft.

21
00:01:29,286 --> 00:01:31,712
That's far too slow to fly it by hand.

22
00:01:32,073 --> 00:01:36,767
So the whole approach and the touch ran
automatically, from commands uploaded in

23
00:01:36,787 --> 00:01:41,829
advance. The spacecraft lowered itself
toward Nightingale and met the surface at

24
00:01:41,849 --> 00:01:43,961
about 10 centimeters per second.

25
00:01:44,194 --> 00:01:46,225
That's about four inches per second.

26
00:01:46,678 --> 00:01:52,169
Here's what happened next, second by second,
in NASA's data-driven reconstruction.

27
00:01:52,460 --> 00:01:57,741
Zero seconds. The round sampler
head touches Nightingale.

28
00:01:57,765 --> 00:01:59,533
Not the spacecraft's body.

29
00:01:59,553 --> 00:02:01,840
Just the head, on the end of its arm.

30
00:02:02,015 --> 00:02:06,518
About one second later, pressurized
nitrogen fires into the surface.

31
00:02:06,647 --> 00:02:09,967
The gas lifts dust and gravel
up toward the collector.

32
00:02:10,420 --> 00:02:12,765
That bright burst in the real photos?

33
00:02:12,893 --> 00:02:15,412
That's material thrown up by the gas.

34
00:02:15,540 --> 00:02:18,187
Not fire, and not an explosion.

35
00:02:18,362 --> 00:02:22,691
At about six seconds, the
back-away thrusters start firing.

36
00:02:22,866 --> 00:02:24,816
But the arm is still sinking.

37
00:02:25,049 --> 00:02:29,592
Only at about nine seconds does the thrust
finally stop the descent, and begin to

38
00:02:29,612 --> 00:02:31,411
carry the spacecraft away.

39
00:02:31,644 --> 00:02:35,869
By then, the sampler's arm had pushed
nearly half a meter into the asteroid.

40
00:02:35,893 --> 00:02:37,703
That's about a foot and a half.

41
00:02:37,832 --> 00:02:41,476
Around sixteen seconds, the arm
comes fully out of the material.

42
00:02:41,930 --> 00:02:47,629
And at about thirty seconds, the thrusters
shut off, and the spacecraft drifts away.

43
00:02:48,180 --> 00:02:50,280
So why so little resistance?

44
00:02:50,641 --> 00:02:55,323
When NASA's team analyzed the data, they
found that the particles on Bennu's surface

45
00:02:55,343 --> 00:02:58,965
are loosely packed, and only
weakly bound to each other.

46
00:02:59,198 --> 00:03:02,691
Push on them, and they don't
push back like solid ground.

47
00:03:02,866 --> 00:03:07,382
They slide out of the way, a lot like
the plastic balls in a kids' ball pit.

48
00:03:07,615 --> 00:03:09,982
NASA's animation shows the idea.

49
00:03:10,076 --> 00:03:13,407
The same weight, arriving
at the same slow speed.

50
00:03:13,582 --> 00:03:15,811
On the Moon, the ground holds.

51
00:03:15,939 --> 00:03:18,980
On Bennu, the grains
give way, and it sinks.

52
00:03:19,155 --> 00:03:22,603
The boulders on top don't mean
there's solid rock underneath.

53
00:03:22,836 --> 00:03:27,529
And according to NASA's analysis, if the
spacecraft had not fired its thrusters to

54
00:03:27,549 --> 00:03:30,149
back away, it would have kept sinking.

55
00:03:30,812 --> 00:03:33,434
That doesn't make Bennu liquid, or hollow.

56
00:03:33,725 --> 00:03:37,869
It means the surface is a loose layer
of particles that barely holds together.

57
00:03:38,102 --> 00:03:40,191
The site itself shows what happened.

58
00:03:40,366 --> 00:03:46,151
The spacecraft photographed Nightingale
on March 7, 2019, and again on April

59
00:03:46,171 --> 00:03:49,723
7, 2021. About two years apart.

60
00:03:50,014 --> 00:03:52,846
Flip between them, and
the patch has changed.

61
00:03:53,021 --> 00:03:54,831
Material has been moved.

62
00:03:55,378 --> 00:04:00,478
NASA described the hollow left at the
site as about eight meters wide, roughly

63
00:04:00,498 --> 00:04:05,737
twenty-six feet. In this elevation model
built from the spacecraft's data, the

64
00:04:05,757 --> 00:04:08,531
colors show how much the
surface height changed.

65
00:04:08,590 --> 00:04:10,590
Blue is where it dropped the most.

66
00:04:10,610 --> 00:04:12,699
But don't credit all of it to the touch.

67
00:04:12,723 --> 00:04:17,637
Three things disturbed this ground: the
sampler head pressing in, the nitrogen gas

68
00:04:17,657 --> 00:04:21,963
blowing into it, and the thrusters
firing as the spacecraft backed away.

69
00:04:22,324 --> 00:04:24,018
Then came a second problem.

70
00:04:24,309 --> 00:04:28,616
Soon after the touch, the spacecraft
photographed its sampler head.

71
00:04:29,069 --> 00:04:33,519
Something was off. Some of the collected
particles were escaping through small gaps

72
00:04:33,539 --> 00:04:37,613
in a flap that was meant to keep
them in, and it was slightly open.

73
00:04:37,788 --> 00:04:42,188
The team's interpretation:
larger rocks had wedged it open.

74
00:04:42,549 --> 00:04:44,057
The next step mattered.

75
00:04:44,290 --> 00:04:47,807
The head, with its sample, was
placed into the return capsule.

76
00:04:47,983 --> 00:04:52,045
And on October 28, 2020,
the capsule was closed.

77
00:04:52,162 --> 00:04:54,622
The spacecraft itself never came home.

78
00:04:54,740 --> 00:04:56,178
Only the capsule did.

79
00:04:56,388 --> 00:04:59,951
It reached Earth on September 24, 2023.

80
00:05:00,161 --> 00:05:05,505
Inside, NASA measured 121.6
grams of asteroid material.

81
00:05:05,525 --> 00:05:07,119
That's about four ounces.

82
00:05:07,139 --> 00:05:09,611
The mission needed at least 60 grams.

83
00:05:09,740 --> 00:05:11,620
It brought back about twice that.

84
00:05:11,853 --> 00:05:13,512
And the sample delivered.

85
00:05:13,873 --> 00:05:19,704
In January 2025, scientists reported
14 of the 20 amino acids that

86
00:05:19,724 --> 00:05:23,439
life on Earth uses, plus five nucleobases.

87
00:05:23,532 --> 00:05:28,547
In December 2025, they reported
sugars, including ribose and glucose.

88
00:05:28,722 --> 00:05:30,556
None of this is evidence of life.

89
00:05:30,684 --> 00:05:32,971
No organisms, no DNA.

90
00:05:33,146 --> 00:05:35,479
These are chemical ingredients.

91
00:05:35,499 --> 00:05:41,168
And it all came from one brief touch,
on a surface so loose that, by NASA's

92
00:05:41,188 --> 00:05:46,144
analysis, without its thrusters the
spacecraft would have kept sinking.

93
00:05:46,807 --> 00:05:51,961
On an asteroid like Bennu, touching
down isn't landing on solid ground.

94
00:05:52,194 --> 00:05:56,686
It's reaching into a loose pile of
rubble, and getting back out in time.
